1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,872
Dana keeps a note on her phone titled "premium bundle, someday": a bigger box, more

2
00:00:04,872 --> 00:00:07,759
socks, a price well past a hundred and fifty dollars.

3
00:00:07,759 --> 00:00:12,063
She has never priced a shoot against it, because at Harlan's actual fifty-eight

4
00:00:12,063 --> 00:00:15,387
dollar AOV, the answer isn't close enough to be worth asking.

5
00:00:15,387 --> 00:00:19,745
Four lectures into "generate more, test honestly", it's time to say exactly why,

6
00:00:19,745 --> 00:00:24,049
because pretending this approach always wins would make the rest of this course

7
00:00:24,049 --> 00:00:24,812
untrustworthy.

8
00:00:24,812 --> 00:00:29,279
There's a number that decides it, and it's not creative quality or brand prestige.

9
00:00:29,279 --> 00:00:33,638
It's the average order value, the AOV: how much a single sale is actually worth.

10
00:00:33,638 --> 00:00:37,942
Get on the wrong side of that number and everything you've learned this section

11
00:00:37,942 --> 00:00:41,102
still works, but it's no longer the best tool for the job.

12
00:00:41,102 --> 00:00:45,351
This matters because you're going to meet people who've heard one half of this

13
00:00:45,351 --> 00:00:49,710
course's argument and turned it into a blanket rule: AI volume always wins, full

14
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:49,982
stop.

15
00:00:49,982 --> 00:00:54,177
That's not what the last four lectures actually showed you, and believing the

16
00:00:54,177 --> 00:00:58,481
blanket version will cost you the one call this section exists to help you make

17
00:00:58,481 --> 00:00:59,026
correctly.

18
00:00:59,576 --> 00:01:04,207
Here's why the same decision comes out differently depending on which column you're

19
00:01:04,207 --> 00:01:04,374
in.

20
00:01:04,374 --> 00:01:08,894
At Harlan's numbers, a proper shoot needs roughly eighty-eight orders just to pay

21
00:01:08,894 --> 00:01:11,795
for itself before it's contributed a cent of profit.

22
00:01:11,795 --> 00:01:16,091
That's a real bet, and if the creative underperforms, it's a bet Harlan loses

23
00:01:16,091 --> 00:01:19,439
slowly, watching margin disappear order by order for months.

24
00:01:19,439 --> 00:01:24,070
At two hundred dollars a sale, the same shoot pays for itself in about twenty-three

25
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:26,692
orders, absorbed inside a couple of good weeks.

26
00:01:26,692 --> 00:01:28,366
The creative is the same cost.

27
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:31,881
The number of times it has to be right is completely different.

28
00:01:31,881 --> 00:01:36,568
Flip it around and the same maths tells you something about risk tolerance, not just

29
00:01:36,568 --> 00:01:37,349
payback speed.

30
00:01:37,349 --> 00:01:41,310
Eighty-eight orders at Harlan's volume could take months to accumulate.

31
00:01:41,310 --> 00:01:45,941
A shoot that flops there doesn't just cost money, it costs time the account doesn't

32
00:01:45,941 --> 00:01:47,671
have to spend finding that out.

33
00:01:48,221 --> 00:01:52,892
Let's name the line properly, because "it depends" isn't useful without the actual

34
00:01:52,892 --> 00:01:54,032
mechanism behind it.

35
00:01:54,032 --> 00:01:58,476
The threshold sits wherever a bigger budget's cost, divided by your margin per

36
00:01:58,476 --> 00:02:01,780
order, stops asking for an uncomfortable number of orders.

37
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:04,572
There's no single dollar figure that's universal.

38
00:02:04,572 --> 00:02:09,358
It moves with your margin, how often people buy, and how considered the purchase is.

39
00:02:09,358 --> 00:02:14,029
What stays constant is the question: how many orders does this spend need, and can

40
00:02:14,029 --> 00:02:17,790
you actually stomach that many going wrong before it turns around?

41
00:02:17,790 --> 00:02:22,347
This is also why the same rule shows up in completely different industries under

42
00:02:22,347 --> 00:02:23,259
different names.

43
00:02:23,259 --> 00:02:27,874
A software company thinking about an enterprise sales team asks a version of this

44
00:02:27,874 --> 00:02:28,387
question.

45
00:02:28,387 --> 00:02:33,001
A publisher deciding whether a book is worth an advance asks a version of it too.

46
00:02:33,001 --> 00:02:34,084
The number changes.

47
00:02:34,084 --> 00:02:37,958
The underlying arithmetic, cost divided by margin per unit, doesn't.

48
00:02:38,508 --> 00:02:42,620
I want to be clear that this isn't AI creative losing to "real" creative.

49
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:46,620
It's two different tools, correctly matched to two different economics.

50
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:51,240
Where the margin per order is thin and purchases happen often, a system that tests

51
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:55,859
cheaply and finds a winner statistically beats one confident, expensive bet, every

52
00:02:55,859 --> 00:02:56,141
time.

53
00:02:56,141 --> 00:03:00,817
Where the margin is generous and the purchase is considered, rare, or carrying real

54
00:03:00,817 --> 00:03:05,549
brand weight, a directed, human-made piece earns back its cost easily and often does

55
00:03:05,549 --> 00:03:10,000
a job volume testing was never built for: building trust in a single, unhurried

56
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,380
moment rather than winning a fraction of a second of scroll.

57
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:17,943
Brand campaigns specifically live on the right side of this table for a different

58
00:03:17,943 --> 00:03:19,520
reason than economics alone.

59
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,914
Their goal usually isn't "which variant converts this week", it's a consistent

60
00:03:23,914 --> 00:03:28,647
impression built over months, and a batch of twenty statistically-optimised variants

61
00:03:28,647 --> 00:03:30,900
actively works against that consistency.

62
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:33,435
That's not a failure of this course's method.

63
00:03:33,435 --> 00:03:36,928
It's a different job with a different success metric entirely.

64
00:03:37,478 --> 00:03:40,618
So where does the account you're following actually sit?

65
00:03:40,618 --> 00:03:41,963
Below the line, clearly.

66
00:03:41,963 --> 00:03:46,616
At fifty-eight dollars, Harlan's whole economics argue for exactly what this course

67
00:03:46,616 --> 00:03:48,635
teaches: cheap, wide, tested volume.

68
00:03:48,635 --> 00:03:53,064
If Dana ever launches a premium bundle, something well past a hundred and fifty

69
00:03:53,064 --> 00:03:57,436
dollars, the maths on this slide starts to argue for a proper shoot around it.

70
00:03:57,436 --> 00:04:02,034
She hasn't built that yet, so for now, treat the volume approach as the right call

71
00:04:02,034 --> 00:04:06,631
on its own merits, not a budget compromise she's settling for until she can afford

72
00:04:06,631 --> 00:04:07,023
better.

73
00:04:07,023 --> 00:04:11,620
Worth saying plainly: nothing about the rest of this course changes if that bundle

74
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:12,573
never gets built.

75
00:04:12,573 --> 00:04:17,171
Everything from Section two onward assumes exactly the account you've already met,

76
00:04:17,171 --> 00:04:19,581
at exactly the numbers you've already seen.

77
00:04:19,581 --> 00:04:24,179
The bundle is a live possibility worth knowing the threshold for, not a plot point

78
00:04:24,179 --> 00:04:26,085
the rest of the course depends on.

79
00:04:26,635 --> 00:04:27,741
That's Section one.

80
00:04:27,741 --> 00:04:32,283
Five ideas, and every one of them has a number attached now instead of a vibe.

81
00:04:32,283 --> 00:04:36,824
Section two turns all of it into the actual document that decides what's worth

82
00:04:36,824 --> 00:04:39,212
generating in the first place: the brief.

83
00:04:39,212 --> 00:04:43,986
It's the difference between twenty real shots at the tail and twenty variations on

84
00:04:43,986 --> 00:04:45,092
the same one guess.

85
00:04:45,092 --> 00:04:46,897
One thing before you get there.

86
00:04:46,897 --> 00:04:51,788
Divide your own product's production cost by its margin per order, the same division

87
00:04:51,788 --> 00:04:53,768
that placed Harlan below the line.

88
00:04:53,768 --> 00:04:55,690
Write down what that asks of you.

89
00:04:55,690 --> 00:05:00,581
It's the first honest input Section 2.6's brief, artifact B01, needs, whichever side

90
00:05:00,581 --> 00:05:01,978
of the line you land on.
